Future Plans:

- In config.yml, add an option to enable Auto Punish instead of simply flagging players. Punishments should be configurable, ranging from kicks to permanent or temporary bans.

- Make flag messages and punishment messages customisable through config.yml. This should include separate messages for kicks, bans and temporary bans.

- Add webhook compatibility for sending alerts and detection notifications to external platforms.

- In the future, shorten the jump sample windows to focus exclusively on jumps occurring around hit events.
- This will help filter out ordinary jumping and focus specifically on jump resets.
  - Use a KeepAlive packet with a randomly generated ID, sent to the client immediately after sending the DamageEvent packet (if the attacker is a player).
  - The client will respond to the KeepAlive packet with the same ID.
  - When the response is received, we can check whether the player performed a jump reset by checking for a Player Input packet in the same tick.
  - If a jump input is detected in that tick, the player performed a jump reset. Otherwise, they did not.
  - This means the sample windows will contain only jump reset attempts rather than ordinary jumps.
    - This will make AJRCheck2 easier to implement.
    - It should also improve AJRCheck accuracy by filtering out unrelated jumps.
  - This change will apply to both AJRCheck and AJRCheck2.

- AJRCheck2 should detect consistently low JumpStartStops values rather than only detecting jumps lasting exactly 1 tick.
- This will allow us to detect clients (cheats) that artificially force their jumps to last longer than 1 tick to bypass existing detection.

- Add a command in the future, such as /JRP (Jump Reset Percentage), with an appropriate permission, allowing admins to view a player's jump reset percentage.

- In the future, collect additional hit information to allow AJR detection to be broken down by hit type.
  - For example, detect jump resets specifically on sprint hits, critical hits, falling sprint hits, or only while holding W.
  - This could help identify whether a client is only performing certain types of jump resets.
  - Potentially incorporate this information into /JRP to provide a more detailed breakdown of a player's jump reset statistics and overall JR skill.